---
export const prerender = true;
import EditorialArticleLayout from '../../components/EditorialArticleLayout.astro';

const slug = 'browser-ai-vs-local-ai-privacy';
const title = 'Browser AI vs Local AI: What Stays on Your Device?';
const description =
  'Compare browser inference, model downloads, cloud question routing, and analytics using the current Access Free Tools implementation and its limits.';
const imagePath = '/social/browser-ai-vs-local-ai-privacy.webp';
const socialImagePath = '/social/browser-ai-vs-local-ai-privacy.png';
const imageAlt =
  'Full-body smoke-kawaii girl comparing a browser window on one side with a small local computer and shield on the other, joined by visible network lights.';
const sources = [
  { href: 'https://github.com/naptha/tesseract.js', label: 'Tesseract.js official repository' },
  { href: 'https://github.com/naptha/tesseract.js/blob/master/docs/api.md', label: 'Tesseract.js worker API and cache options' },
  { href: 'https://tesseract-ocr.github.io/tessdoc/ImproveQuality.html', label: 'Tesseract: image quality limits' },
  { href: 'https://huggingface.co/docs/transformers.js/index', label: 'Hugging Face: Transformers.js' },
  { href: 'https://huggingface.co/docs/transformers.js/custom_usage', label: 'Hugging Face: model and runtime locations' },
  { href: 'https://docs.ollama.com/api/introduction', label: 'Ollama API introduction' },
  { href: 'https://docs.ollama.com/cloud', label: 'Ollama cloud modes' },
  { href: 'https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Fetch_API', label: 'MDN: Fetch API' },
  { href: 'https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/clarity/setup-and-installation/clarity-masking', label: 'Microsoft: Clarity content masking' },
];
const relatedLinks = [
  {
    href: '/categories/ai-tools/',
    label: 'Browser AI tools',
    description: 'Find AI helpers with model, download, and accuracy limits.',
  },
  {
    href: '/tools/image-to-text-ocr-tool/',
    label: 'Image to Text OCR Tool',
    description: 'Try printed-text recognition with a selected image.',
  },
  {
    href: '/blog/browser-text-to-speech-kokoro-vs-supertonic/',
    label: 'Kokoro and Supertonic browser speech',
    description: 'Compare model downloads, runtime choices, licenses, and maintenance limits.',
  },
  {
    href: '/ask/',
    label: 'Ask Access Free Tools',
    description: 'See how the server routes questions to deterministic tools.',
  },
  {
    href: '/privacy-policy/',
    label: 'Privacy policy',
    description: 'Check the current analytics, forms, and AI-routing details.',
  },
];
---

<EditorialArticleLayout
  slug={slug}
  title={title}
  seoTitle="Browser AI vs Local AI Privacy: What Stays on Your Device?"
  description={description}
  breadcrumbLabel="Browser AI versus local AI privacy"
  imagePath={imagePath}
  socialImagePath={socialImagePath}
  imageAlt={imageAlt}
  imageCaption="The smoke-kawaii mascot checks where processing happens, which files download, and whether content crosses the network."
  sources={sources}
  relatedLinks={relatedLinks}
>
  <Fragment slot="intro">
    <p>
      A browser can turn a receipt into text without uploading the image for recognition.
      That same page can still download code, record a tool action, or call a cloud service for a
      different task. To understand privacy, follow the input and the requests separately.
      Access Free Tools provides examples of all these routes.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <section class="content-section article-flow-section">
    <h2>Quick answer</h2>
    <p>
      Browser AI runs inference or recognition inside a browser tab. Local AI often runs through
      installed software on your computer. Either can process input on the device, but neither
      label proves the entire workflow is offline. Model downloads, cloud fallbacks, analytics,
      and the surrounding application can make separate requests.
    </p>
    <p>
      Check four things: where the input goes, where processing runs, which support files download,
      and what other traffic the page sends. The examples below describe the Access Free Tools
      implementation reviewed on October 5, 2026. They are not a guarantee for every browser,
      setting, extension, or future version.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section article-flow-section">
    <h2>Browser processing does not mean zero network traffic</h2>
    <p class="article-source-note">
      Source: <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Fetch_API" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">MDN's Fetch API guide</a>
    </p>
    <p>
      A web page can download JavaScript, WebAssembly, language data, model weights, and images
      before it can work. Those downloads connect to the servers supplying the files. Browser
      code can also send data through fetch requests, forms, beacons, or third-party scripts.
    </p>
    <p>
      A model download is different from sending a receipt or pasted text to a server for
      inference. Both are network activity. A useful privacy statement names the content,
      processing step, destination, and any separate telemetry.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section article-flow-section">
    <h2>OCR passes the selected image to a browser worker</h2>
    <p class="article-source-note">
      Sources: <a href="https://github.com/naptha/tesseract.js" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Tesseract.js</a>
      and its <a href="https://github.com/naptha/tesseract.js/blob/master/docs/api.md" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">worker API</a>
    </p>
    <p>
      The <a href="/tools/image-to-text-ocr-tool/">Image to Text OCR Tool</a> reads the selected file
      into image bytes and passes them to a Tesseract.js web worker. Its recognition code does not
      upload the image to Access Free Tools. The worker, WebAssembly core, and selected language
      data load from the site's own model directory after Read text is pressed.
    </p>
    <p>
      Six language files are supplied: English, Spanish, French, German, Italian, and Portuguese.
      Language data can be cached in the browser, so a later run may make fewer requests. A clear
      English <a href="/samples/ocr-synthetic-english.png" download="ocr-synthetic-english.png">synthetic sample</a>
      has been recognized and copied in a local Chromium test with external requests blocked.
      That proves that tested English path can work with locally served assets. It does not prove
      every language, image, browser, or live-site session stays offline.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href="https://tesseract-ocr.github.io/tessdoc/ImproveQuality.html" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Image quality affects recognition.</a>
      OCR can still misread names, dates, totals, and account numbers. Compare the output
      with the original before using it. The <a href="/blog/how-to-use-image-to-text-ocr-tool/">OCR guide</a>
      shows the sample workflow and file limits.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section article-flow-section">
    <h2>Model locations and runtime locations are separate settings</h2>
    <p class="article-source-note">
      Sources: <a href="https://huggingface.co/docs/transformers.js/index" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Transformers.js</a>
      and its <a href="https://huggingface.co/docs/transformers.js/custom_usage" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">custom model and runtime settings</a>
    </p>
    <p>
      This site's sentiment and tone classifiers request self-hosted MobileBERT model files with
      <code>local_files_only: true</code>. Here, local means the site's model directory. The browser
      still downloads those files before running inference. It does not mean the files were
      already installed on the visitor's device.
    </p>
    <p>
      That model-loading option does not configure WebAssembly runtime downloads. Transformers.js
      documents separate model and WASM locations; disabling remote models does not disable every
      runtime request. This site's shared classifier loader leaves the WASM location unset, so
      local model loading alone is not evidence of a completely self-hosted or offline runtime.
    </p>
    <p>
      The summarizer and image classifier can request models from a remote host. The browser speech
      and transcription workers also request their selected pinned model files from Hugging Face.
      Their current processing code passes text or decoded media to browser workers or pipelines,
      rather than uploading that content for server inference. Downloads can recur after a cache
      is cleared or a model changes. Check the actual request payloads before using sensitive input.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section article-flow-section">
    <h2>A localhost API can still route a prompt to the cloud</h2>
    <p class="article-source-note">
      Sources: <a href="https://docs.ollama.com/api/introduction" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Ollama API</a>
      and <a href="https://docs.ollama.com/cloud" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Ollama cloud modes</a>
    </p>
    <p>
      Ollama supports a local API at <code>http://localhost:11434/api</code> and a hosted API at
      <code>https://ollama.com/api</code>. A local Ollama server can also use cloud models.
      Seeing localhost in an application's settings proves the first connection is local,
      not that inference happens on your computer. Check the selected model and cloud settings.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href="/ask/">Ask Access Free Tools</a> first sends every submitted question to the
      Access Free Tools server. The server tries its supported parser. If no route is found and
      cloud routing is configured, it can send the question to Ollama's hosted chat API.
      A parser answer avoids that provider call, but the question still reached the site's server.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section article-flow-section">
    <h2>A correct answer does not prove a private route</h2>
    <p>
      For example, "What is 15% of 80?" has the exact answer 12. The Ask parser can select the
      percentage calculator and its inputs, then the deterministic runner supplies the answer.
      Ollama can help route other supported requests, but it does not supply the final arithmetic.
    </p>
    <p>
      The tool page, REST API, and MCP endpoint use the same calculation logic. That makes the
      result repeatable. It says nothing by itself about where a question was sent. Verify the
      processing route and the result as two separate checks.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section article-flow-section">
    <h2>Analytics and session replay have their own boundaries</h2>
    <p class="article-source-note">
      Source: <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/clarity/setup-and-installation/clarity-masking" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Microsoft's Clarity masking documentation</a>
    </p>
    <p>
      Access Free Tools sends first-party page-view and tool-action events separately from model
      processing. Those events include page and tool details, browser-generated visitor and
      session identifiers, language, and screen size. The server hashes identifiers and the
      network address. Hashing does not justify a promise that all usage data is anonymous.
    </p>
    <p>
      Microsoft Clarity can also load on public production pages. The tool and Ask interaction
      areas are explicitly marked for masking, including inputs and generated results. Masking
      protects content in Clarity recordings; it does not stop every network request or make the
      entire page invisible to analytics.
    </p>
    <p>
      The <a href="/privacy-policy/">privacy policy</a> provides an Exclude this browser button.
      After choosing it, reload the page: the site's opt-out prevents first-party analytics and
      Clarity from loading or sending events on the next page load. The code also honors Do Not
      Track and excludes private admin pages. Local browser tests check these boundaries without
      sending telemetry to production services; they are not a live production traffic audit.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section article-flow-section">
    <h2>A practical checklist for checking a privacy claim</h2>
    <ul class="editorial-checklist">
      <li><strong>Read the exact page.</strong> Look for input, model, analytics, and fallback details.</li>
      <li><strong>Inspect the Network panel.</strong> Clear it, use a non-sensitive sample, run one task, and inspect new requests and payloads.</li>
      <li><strong>Compare first and later runs.</strong> A warm cache can hide downloads that a new visitor will receive.</li>
      <li><strong>Check the processing route.</strong> A button can call a worker, localhost, the site's server, or a remote provider. Localhost can forward requests.</li>
      <li><strong>Try a disconnected run.</strong> Success proves only that the tested path worked with the files already available. A missing asset may still prevent another task.</li>
      <li><strong>Repeat after changing settings.</strong> A language, model, cloud toggle, or analytics preference can change traffic.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Browser requests are one part of the picture. Extensions, operating-system services, and
      local applications can have separate traffic. A single successful test cannot establish
      how every part of a device handles data.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section article-flow-section">
    <h2>Use a privacy claim that names the actual task</h2>
    <p>
      "This image is processed in the browser and is not uploaded to Access Free Tools for OCR"
      describes a specific recognition path. "This AI tool is private" leaves the downloads,
      analytics, and any other routes unexplained. Prefer a claim you can check against the task
      you are about to run.
    </p>
    <p>
      Compare the <a href="/categories/ai-tools/">AI tools hub</a>, the
      <a href="/privacy-policy/">privacy policy</a>, and each tool's limits before choosing where
      to put sensitive material. The <a href="/blog/open-source-projects-behind-access-free-tools/">open-source stack article</a>
      explains how the site's components fit together.
    </p>
  </section>
</EditorialArticleLayout>
